fact WorkItem(id: "WI-764", description: some(value: "An operation annotating its return as an explicit Relation[T = .., E = ..] does not conform against a rule citation, though both render the SAME bindings. REPRO (loads clean minus this op): rule person_row(?name, ?age) :- person(name: ?name, age: ?age); operation src() -> Relation[E = {Error}, T = (name: String, age: Int64)] = person_row -- fails with 'type mismatch in src.return (op-return): expected Relation[E = {merge[left = present[label = Error], right = empty_row]}, T = (name: String, age: Int64)], got Relation[T = (name: String, age: Int64), E = {merge[left = present[label = Error], right = empty_row]}]'. The two sides differ ONLY in the ORDER the bindings render (E,T vs T,E); writing the annotation in either order gives the same failure, so it is not the surface order that matters -- the DECLARED side normalizes one way and the rule-citation side (relation_type_from_columns -> assemble_relation_type, which fills bindings in sort-param order) the other. PRE-EXISTING, measured not assumed: reproduced byte-identically on e86e4dda (pre-WI-732) in a clean worktree, with a source containing NO dot expression and NO projection, so none of WI-732's paths are involved. FOUND WHILE trying to construct a receiver shape for WI-732's new hardening diagnostic --  -- which this blocks, so the hardening branch is currently unreachable-by-measurement and untested (59 recognizer hits across anthill-core, 0 fall-throughs). ACCEPTANCE: the repro loads; conformance for a parameterized sort compares bindings BY NAME rather than depending on either producer's fill order (or the two producers are made to agree, with the reason recorded); a regression test pins an annotated Relation return in BOTH binding orders; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-19T00:11:42Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-764", author: "user", content: "Description note: the shape that led here, elided by a shell quoting slip when this item was filed, is the call-result receiver `src()` followed by a distribute-dot projection of `name` and `age` — i.e. projecting directly off a call result with no intervening `let`. That is the shape WI-732`s hardening branch would need in order to be reachable and testable.", at: "2026-07-18T21:02:19Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-764", author: "user", content: "RENUMBERED WI-762 -> WI-764 on merge with origin/main. This ticket did not collide directly, but was shifted to keep the three locally-filed items contiguous after WI-760/WI-761 moved to WI-762/WI-763. NOTE commit 9be1f834 still reads \"filed as WI-762\" for this ticket.", at: "2026-07-18T21:30:26Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-764", author: "user", content: "ROOT CAUSE was NOT binding order — measured, not reasoned. Instrumenting parameterized_compatible_view showed the two sides key the SAME slot with DIFFERENT symbols: the rule citation uses the sort's canonical param symbols (anthill.prelude.Relation.T = 1352, .E = 1353), the written annotation uses BARE ones (T = 2337, E = 2331). The subtype relation matched bindings by raw symbol identity, so neither slot was ever found; same-base means no provider fallback, so it rejected. The render order differed for the same reason and is only a FINGERPRINT: make_entity_term's canonicalizer sorts named args by symbol INDEX, and the two symbol sets intern in opposite relative order. This is WI-726's bug exactly, in the sibling direction — WI-726 fixed the UNIFY twin and left the SUBTYPE twin on raw identity, despite commit 0f31beb2 having consolidated the pair specifically to keep them in lockstep. FIX: one shared binding_for_param / binding_index_for_param keyed by a BindingKeyMatch{Label,Identity} derived from the two bases (label-match within one sort, identity across), replacing the raw-identity lookup in BOTH directions. Identity is tried first across the whole slice, label only on miss, so an exact key always wins over a merely same-labelled one.", at: "2026-07-19T00:10:31Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-764", author: "user", content: "TWO FURTHER DEFECTS the fix exposed, both found by /code-review and both MEASURED before being fixed. (1) DUPLICATE PARAM KEY: with conformance fixed, 'Relation[T = <right>, T = <wrong>, E = {Error}]' loaded with ZERO errors while the same two bindings in the opposite order rejected — binding lookup is first-wins, so a stale correct binding above a new wrong one satisfied the check and the wrong one was never compared. check_sort_type_args admitted it because it only asked whether each key is DECLARED, never whether it appears once. Now a loud TypeArgProblem::DuplicateParam at that gate (scoped to a Sort head, like the rest of that check — NOT universal). Note this defeats a naive negative test: prefixing a correct duplicate hides a wrong schema. (2) PARTIAL ANNOTATION DUPLICATED A SLOT from ORDINARY source: unroll_annotation_with_inferred merged annotation and value bindings by raw identity, so 'let r : Relation[E = {Error}] = person_row' built Relation[T = .., E = .., E = ..] — one slot bound twice, the annotation's bare E and the value's canonical Relation.E. Now merges through the shared key rule. Both have regression tests.", at: "2026-07-19T00:10:49Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-764", author: "user", content: "FALSIFIED PREDICTION + WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY LEFT. WI-732's hardening commit (9be1f834) called its unresolvable-receiver branch 'unreachable by measurement, therefore untested' and named this ticket as the blocker, expecting a projection off a call result to reach it once the annotation conformed. It does NOT: 'src().(age, name)' now types CLEANLY, because that branch fires only when projection_receiver_type cannot resolve the receiver and a call result resolves fine. So unblocking WI-764 did not make it reachable; it stays untested. (Measured for THIS shape only.) The regression test asserts the diagnostic's absence as a tripwire. LEFT UN-ENROLLED, deliberately, each a same-base guard followed by a raw-identity binding lookup — same defect class, but each is a behavior change in a subsystem with no failing case here: (a) match_candidate_against_goal (typing.rs ~12639/12645) — dispatch specificity; now ASYMMETRIC with the typer (subtype accepts the canonical-vs-bare pair, dispatch still drops the candidate), and enrolling it changes overload ORDERING via specificity, so it is not a free follow-up; (b) combine_parameterized_same_base (typing.rs ~24634/24650) — the LUB/GLB lattice; on a key miss returns the bare sort / nothing, silently dropping a schema. A reviewer built five if-join probes and could NOT demonstrate it, and instrumentation shows its binding matcher is executed by ZERO existing tests. Both named in binding_for_param's doc.", at: "2026-07-19T00:11:04Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-764", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED: conformance for a parameterized sort compares bindings by the shared key rule (label within one sort, identity across sorts) instead of raw symbol identity, in BOTH the subtype and unify directions via one binding_for_param/binding_index_for_param. Repro loads in both binding orders; 7 regression tests incl. both orders, two non-vacuity controls, the duplicate-param guard in both orders, the partial-annotation slot, and the projection-off-a-call-result shape; full workspace 3083 passed / 0 failed via scripts/test.sh; /code-review run at max effort (10 finder angles), findings applied — the two extra defects above were found there.", at: "2026-07-19T00:11:40Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-764", author: "user", content: "Un-enrolled siblings filed as WI-768 (match_candidate_against_goal, dispatch — now asymmetric with the typer; enrolling also moves overload ORDERING via specificity) and WI-769 (combine_parameterized_same_base, LUB/GLB lattice — measured INERT: five if-join probes could not demonstrate it and its binding matcher is executed by zero tests, so the open question there is reachability, not the patch). binding_for_param's doc now cites both by number.", at: "2026-07-19T04:59:02Z")
